<section xml:id="sec-ws-02-blind-pass">
  <title>Worksheet 2: Blind Episode Pass</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>Produce an independent record of the justification visible in each assigned response.</li>
      <li>Screen and segment responses without access to codebook identities or machine proposals.</li>
      <li>Record criteria, inferential links, judgments, completeness, and episode relations without repairing the response.</li>
      <li>Document uncertainty and alternative readings before comparison or review.</li>
      <li>Lock the first-pass record so later assistance cannot erase the unaided analysis.</li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This worksheet is the second milestone in the researcher training sequence. It
      measures how you interpret short mathematical justifications before formal
      categories or machine suggestions can influence that interpretation. Your task is
      to preserve the episode structure visible in the response, not to assign a
      control-structure code or decide whether the pipeline would agree.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is an <term>independent</term> milestone. Complete the assigned batch without
      consulting another coder, the project codebook, the controlled vocabulary, or any
      pipeline output. Questions about instructions or missing materials may be sent to
      the research lead. Questions about how another person interpreted a particular
      response must wait until the comparison or review stage.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-ws-02-status">
      <title>Milestone status</title>
      <p>
        Completing this worksheet does not authorize formal coding of project data. It
        establishes whether you can produce a stable, auditable episode extraction
        without categorical or machine anchoring. Advancement is based on the preserved
        first-pass record and the review described at the end of this worksheet.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark xml:id="rem-ws-02-governing-instrument">
      <title>Governing instrument (adjudicated 2026-07-28)</title>
      <p>
        The ratified blind-pass instrument is the assigned student-tasks packet:
        Exercise 2 Part A, the supplied blind-pass sheet template, and the Coder's
        Handbook Part I. This worksheet is a derived milestone guide, not a second
        instrument. Where this worksheet and the assigned packet differ — in field
        lists, recorded values, or reading and access restrictions — the packet
        governs. The Coding Protocol <m>\pi</m> is released only after the blind
        pass and does not govern it; the pass is deliberately protocol-light.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-01-overview">
    <title>Milestone at a Glance</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-01-overview">
        <title>Purpose and output of the onboarding milestone.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
            <col width="20%"/>
            <col width="80%"/>
            <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                    <cell><p>Item</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Description</p></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><p>Purpose</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Learn the <xref ref="g-evidence-boundary" text="title"/> and practice episode coding.</p></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><p>Work-mode</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Independent first attempt followed by review and feedback.</p></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><p>Data</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Constructed responses only. No real data is used.</p></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><p>Support</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Assigned handbook sections, worked examples, personal notes, and discussion at meetings.</p></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell><p>Deliverable</p></cell>
              <cell><p>A completed worksheet with the original attempt, corrections, and brief evidence-based rationales.</p></cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-02-overview">
    <title>Milestone at a Glance</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-02-overview">
      <title>Purpose and output of the blind-pass milestone.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="22%"/>
        <col width="78%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Item</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Description</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>Primary purpose</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Capture unaided screening, segmentation, and episode interpretation before formal matching begins.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>Work mode</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Independent, codebook-blind, and machine-blind.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>Data</p></cell>
            <cell><p>The assigned blind-pass packet. Use only the question contexts and responses supplied for this milestone.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>Primary deliverable</p></cell>
            <cell><p>A locked first-pass record containing one row per identified episode, own-words rules and invented category labels with a personal category legend, confidence, and evidence-based notes.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>Not produced</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Control-structure codes, goal coverage, dispositions, or reviews of machine proposals. The two correctness digits <em>are</em> recorded for complete episodes: they evaluate the mathematics against the question and require no codebook.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p>Next milestone</p></cell>
            <cell><p>The codebook-assisted pass, after the research lead confirms that the blind record is complete and properly preserved.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-02-prerequisites">
    <title>Prerequisites and Assigned Reading</title>

    <p>
      Begin only after the research lead has marked Worksheet 1 complete and supplied
      the blind-pass packet, coding sheet, and governing version labels. Review the
      following sections before opening the response packet:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="subsec-episode-components" text="title"/>,
          <xref ref="subsec-episode-explicit-implicit-absent" text="title"/>,
          <xref ref="subsec-episode-complete-incomplete" text="title"/>, and
          <xref ref="subsec-episode-no-repair" text="title"/> in
          <xref ref="ch-episode-model" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="subsec-coding-context" text="title"/> and
          <xref ref="subsec-coding-independent-pass" text="title"/> in
          <xref ref="ch-coding-workflow" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Steps 1–5 and Step 9 in <xref ref="sec-coding-pass" text="title"/>:
          screening, segmentation, transcription, link recording, episode relations,
          and confidence.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="subsec-decision-screening" text="title"/>,
          <xref ref="subsec-decision-segmentation" text="title"/>, and
          <xref ref="subsec-decision-interpretation" text="title"/> in
          <xref ref="ch-edge-cases-decisions" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="subsec-app-coding-sheet-response-fields" text="title"/> and
          <xref ref="subsec-app-coding-sheet-episode-fields" text="title"/> in
          <xref ref="app-coding-sheet" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      Do not read <xref ref="app-protocol-summary" text="title"/> before or during
      the blind pass: it summarizes coding-protocol rules that are released only
      with the assisted recode, and the blind measurement depends on your not having
      them yet.
    </p>

    <p>
      The reading provides rules, not answers to the assigned responses. Do not search
      the handbook, repository, or earlier exercises for wording that resembles a
      response in the blind packet.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-02-access">
    <title>Materials and Access Rules</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-02-access">
      <title>Resources available and withheld during the blind pass.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="50%"/>
        <col width="50%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>You may use</p></cell>
            <cell><p>You must not use</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>The exact question, representations, goals, and normative context supplied in the packet</p></cell>
            <cell><p>The project codebook, code names, code descriptions, or <c>CS1</c>–<c>CS10</c> labels</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>The response text and stable identifiers supplied in the packet</p></cell>
            <cell><p>The controlled vocabulary or lists of preferred mathematical terms</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>The Episode Model, Coding Workflow, edge-case rules, and assigned coding-sheet dictionary</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Another coder's sheet, notes, interpretation, or advice about a response</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>Your own notes created before opening the assigned packet</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Pipeline screening, extraction, matching, confidence, or disposition output</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>The research lead for questions about instructions, missing context, identifiers, or approved access</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Public search, external AI systems, unapproved cloud tools, or real-response examples outside the packet</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p>The approved project folder and assigned coding application</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Private copies, screenshots, personal notes applications, or local renaming of supplied identifiers</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <warning xml:id="warn-ws-02-independence">
      <title>Protect the blind record</title>
      <p>
        The blind pass is a study control. Seeing a code name, another coder's decision,
        or a machine proposal before submission changes the evidence this milestone is
        intended to produce. If accidental exposure occurs, stop, preserve what you had
        completed, and report the exposure to the research lead. Do not conceal the
        incident or continue as though the pass remained blind.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-02-packet">
    <title>Check the Assigned Packet</title>

    <p>
      Before coding any response, verify that the packet provides enough context for an
      independent reading. Record the supplied values in the manifest below. Do not
      invent missing identifiers or privately repair inconsistent materials.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-02-packet-manifest">
      <title>Blind-pass packet manifest.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="32%"/>
        <col width="68%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Required item</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Value or confirmation</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>Batch identifier</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Record the supplied blind-pass batch ID.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>Question identifier(s)</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Record every supplied <c>question_id</c>.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>Response identifiers</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Confirm that every response has one stable <c>response_id</c>.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>Question context</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Confirm that the exact prompt and relevant representation are present.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>Normative context</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Confirm that the agreed mathematical state needed to interpret the response is present.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>Requested goals</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Confirm that the packet identifies what the question asks the student to address.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>Assigned schema</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Record the coding-sheet or database template supplied for the pass.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p>Governing versions</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Record the worksheet, handbook, and protocol versions supplied with the assignment.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <warning xml:id="warn-ws-02-stop-context">
      <title>Stop when the packet is incomplete</title>
      <p>
        Stop and contact the research lead when the prompt, mathematical representation,
        normative context, response text, or stable identifiers are missing, corrupted,
        or internally inconsistent. A private correction would create a different coding
        task from the one assigned.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-02-fields">
    <title>Fields for This Pass</title>

    <p>
      Use the assigned coding schema. Complete only the fields authorized below. If the
      physical sheet uses different column names, follow the supplied mapping rather
      than creating new columns.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-02-fields">
      <title>Columns of the blind-pass template.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="29%"/>
        <col width="20%"/>
        <col width="51%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Field or field group</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Action</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Blind-pass rule</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p><c>response_id</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Complete</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Copy supplied identifiers exactly.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p><c>episode_n</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Complete when applicable</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Number episodes in the order their judgments appear. Use the assigned stub-row convention when no episode is identifiable.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p><c>own_words_rule</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Complete</p></cell>
            <cell><p>The heart of the blind pass. One sentence in your own plain words: <q>The student decides ___ because ___.</q> Reconstructed material belongs here, never in the verbatim fields.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p><c>my_category</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Complete</p></cell>
            <cell><p>A short label you invent for the kind of rule. Reuse your own labels when the same kind recurs, and keep a personal legend defining each label.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p><c>criterion_verbatim</c>, <c>judgment_verbatim</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Complete</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Copy visible evidence. Leave missing components missing.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p><c>link_stated</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Complete</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Classify the link as <c>explicit</c>, <c>implicit</c>, or <c>absent</c>. The blind template has no separate reconstructed-link column; your reading of the rule lives in <c>own_words_rule</c>.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p><c>complete</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Complete</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Completeness depends on identifiable <m>C</m> and <m>J</m>. An absent link alone does not make an episode incomplete.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p><c>c1</c>, <c>c2</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Complete for complete episodes</p></cell>
            <cell><p>The two-digit correctness code per the Coder's Handbook. The digits evaluate the mathematics against the question and require no codebook. Leave blank for incomplete episodes.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p><c>relation</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Complete when applicable</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Record <c>chained(k)</c> or <c>independent</c>; leave blank for a lone episode. Preserve every defensible episode and relation.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p><c>oov_note</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Complete when applicable</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Copy off-standard terminology verbatim. Do not translate it into preferred course language.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p><c>confidence</c>, <c>notes</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Complete</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Record confidence in your interpretation and identify the source of uncertainty.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p><c>code</c>, <c>alt_codes</c>, <c>goal_coverage</c>, and all screening, disposition, review, and version columns</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Not present</p></cell>
            <cell><p>The blind template deliberately carries no codebook-shaped, routing, or comparison columns. The recode columns are appended only in the later assisted pass; do not add them now. Governing versions are preserved in the submission version record, not on coding rows.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Screening has no dedicated column in the blind template: every assigned response
      is represented by at least one row, and a response with no identifiable episode
      receives the assigned stub-row convention with the visible basis recorded in
      <c>notes</c>.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-02-procedure">
    <title>Blind-Pass Procedure</title>

    <p>
      Process one complete response at a time. Do not begin by scanning the packet for
      recurring words or likely categories.
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Read the context.</term> Read the exact prompt, representation,
          normative context, and requested goals before reading the response.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Read the entire response.</term> Do not code from an isolated phrase or
          stop after recognizing one familiar mathematical statement.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Screen.</term> Decide whether the response contains or plausibly
          contains analyzable mathematical justification. Record a brief rationale.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Segment.</term> Identify every defensible criterion-to-judgment move.
          Connector words may help, but they do not determine episode boundaries.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Transcribe.</term> Record the criterion and judgment from the student's
          wording before writing anything in your own words.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Record the rule in your own words.</term> Complete
          <c>own_words_rule</c> and <c>my_category</c>, then classify
          <c>link_stated</c> as explicit, implicit, or absent. When no justification
          is recoverable, mark it absent; do not invent one.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Record structure.</term> Mark completeness and the relation among
          episodes. Preserve alternative segmentations in notes rather than replacing
          the primary record.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Record confidence.</term> Assign confidence to the coding decision and
          identify the precise source of uncertainty when the decision is arguable or
          unresolved.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Check the row against the response.</term> Confirm that verbatim fields
          contain only source evidence and that no missing reason or conclusion has been
          supplied from the normative reference.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Continue independently.</term> Record questions for the research lead
          under the rules below; do not seek another coder's interpretation.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-02-assignment">
    <title>Assignment</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-02-manifest">
      <title>Part A: Verify the Batch</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Complete the packet manifest in <xref ref="tab-ws-02-packet-manifest"/>
          before coding. Report any missing or inconsistent item and wait for corrected
          materials when the issue changes the task.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-02-screen">
      <title>Part B: Screen Every Response</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Represent every assigned <c>response_id</c> by at least one row. A
          mathematically incorrect response still receives episode analysis. Blanks
          and bare answers still get a row: use the assigned stub-row convention and
          record a one-sentence note tied to visible evidence.
        </p>

        <p>
          Preserve uncertain cases for review. Do not exclude a response merely because
          its language is unfamiliar, compressed, or difficult to parse.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-02-extract">
      <title>Part C: Extract and Relate Episodes</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For every response that continues to episode analysis:
          <ol>
            <li>identify every defensible episode;</li>
            <li>number episodes in the order their judgments appear;</li>
            <li>copy each criterion and judgment into its verbatim field;</li>
            <li>write the own-words rule and your invented category label;</li>
            <li>classify the link as <c>explicit</c>, <c>implicit</c>, or <c>absent</c>;</li>
            <li>record episode completeness;</li>
            <li>record the two correctness digits for complete episodes;</li>
            <li>record <c>chained(k)</c> or <c>independent</c> relations, leaving the field blank for a lone episode; and</li>
            <li>preserve a defensible alternative segmentation in <c>notes</c>.</li>
          </ol>
        </p>

        <p>
          Do not assign control-structure codes and do not evaluate overall goal
          coverage in this milestone; those columns are appended only in the later
          assisted pass.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-02-uncertainty">
      <title>Part D: Document Uncertainty</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assign a confidence value to every recorded analysis. For each
          <c>confidence</c>=1 or <c>confidence</c>=2 decision, write a concise note that
          identifies the uncertainty as one or more of the following:
          <ul>
            <li>screening;</li>
            <li>episode boundary;</li>
            <li>criterion;</li>
            <li>inferential link;</li>
            <li>judgment;</li>
            <li>episode relation;</li>
            <li>unfamiliar terminology;</li>
            <li>missing question context; or</li>
            <li>current policy.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
          State the visible evidence for your primary reading and the strongest
          alternative. Do not write only <q>unclear</q> or <q>not sure.</q>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-02-integrity-check">
      <title>Part E: Perform the Integrity Check</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Before submission, search the completed record for fields or text that would
          show accidental assistance. Confirm that:
          <ul>
            <li>no <c>CS</c> labels or codebook names appear;</li>
            <li>no machine proposal has been copied or paraphrased;</li>
            <li>no note cites another coder's interpretation;</li>
            <li>no codebook-dependent or review-dependent columns have been added;</li>
            <li>verbatim fields preserve the student's wording; and</li>
            <li>the first-pass timestamps and governing versions are present in the submission version record (<xref ref="tab-ws-02-version-record"/>), not as added columns on coding rows.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-02-lock">
      <title>Part F: Lock the First-Pass Record</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Submit the completed file through the approved project location and follow the
          supplied procedure for marking the pass <c>locked</c>. After submission:
          <ul>
            <li>do not overwrite or silently revise the blind record;</li>
            <li>do not replace it with a corrected copy under the same filename;</li>
            <li>record later corrections in the assigned review or override fields; and</li>
            <li>retain the original analysis even when later discussion changes your decision.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-02-questions">
    <title>Questions, Interruptions, and Escalation</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-02-questions">
      <title>How to handle questions without compromising independence.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="31%"/>
        <col width="69%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Situation</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Required action</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>A worksheet instruction is ambiguous</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Ask the research lead for clarification. Record the clarification if it changes how the whole batch is processed.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>The question context or response text appears incomplete</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Stop on that item and report the missing material. Do not reconstruct it privately.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>Two readings of a response are defensible</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Record a primary reading, the alternative, and reduced confidence. Continue without consulting another coder.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>You want to know how another coder handled the case</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Record the question and wait until the comparison stage.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>You accidentally see a codebook entry, another coder's work, or machine output</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Stop, preserve the work completed before exposure, and report the incident immediately.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>You discover a possible data-access or privacy problem</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Stop work and follow the incident procedure in <xref ref="subsec-data-incident" text="title"/>.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p>You become fatigued or notice inconsistent decisions</p></cell>
            <cell><p>End the session, record the stopping point, and resume later. Do not silently rewrite earlier rows.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      See <xref ref="subsec-data-useful-question" text="title"/> for the form of a
      useful analytic question and <xref ref="rem-coding-no-silent-revision" text="title"/>
      for the rule governing later discoveries of drift.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-02-deliverables">
    <title>Deliverables</title>

    <p>
      Submit the following through the approved project location:
      <ol>
        <li>the completed packet manifest;</li>
        <li>one row for every defensible episode, plus the assigned stub record for responses with no episode, so that every assigned response is represented;</li>
        <li>the personal category legend: each <c>my_category</c> label with a one-line definition and a count of uses;</li>
        <li>confidence and evidence-based uncertainty notes;</li>
        <li>a brief incident note for any exposure, missing context, or access problem;</li>
        <li>the integrity-check confirmation; and</li>
        <li>the locked first-pass file with the version record below.</li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-02-version-record">
      <title>Version information to preserve with the blind-pass submission.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="34%"/>
        <col width="66%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Field</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Entry</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p><c>worksheet_version</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>ws-02-blind-pass-v0.1</c></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p><c>pass_type</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>blind_episode</c></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p><c>batch_id</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Record the identifier supplied with the packet.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p><c>handbook_version</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Record the version supplied with the assignment.</p></cell>
        </row>
