            <cell><p>Record when the first-pass record was submitted and locked.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-02-checklist">
    <title>Completion Checklist</title>

    <p>
      Before locking the submission, confirm that:
      <ul>
        <li>the packet manifest is complete and the supplied context is internally consistent;</li>
        <li>every response is represented by at least one row, with stub rows and evidence-based notes for responses with no identifiable episode;</li>
        <li>every defensible episode is preserved under the current record-everything rule;</li>
        <li>criteria and judgments are copied from the response when present;</li>
        <li>reconstructed material appears only in the own-words rule and notes, never in verbatim fields;</li>
        <li>missing criteria or judgments have not been repaired;</li>
        <li>link status and completeness are recorded as separate decisions;</li>
        <li>alternative segmentations and readings are recorded in notes rather than substituted silently;</li>
        <li>every low-confidence decision identifies the source of uncertainty;</li>
        <li>correctness digits are recorded for complete episodes only, and no codebook, coverage, disposition, or review columns have been added;</li>
        <li>no other coder's work or machine proposal influenced the record;</li>
        <li>any accidental exposure or data incident has been reported;</li>
        <li>identifiers and student wording have not been normalized or renamed;</li>
        <li>the governing versions and timestamps are present; and</li>
        <li>the submitted first-pass record is locked against silent revision.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-02-review">
    <title>Review and Advancement</title>

    <p>
      The research lead first checks the submission for procedural integrity: complete
      identifiers, authorized fields, preserved first-pass evidence, and compliance with
      blind-access rules. Analytic review begins only after the record is locked.
    </p>

    <p>
      The review should examine the earliest decision that produced each difference
      between the blind record and the current reference analysis. A later disagreement
      about the final episode description may originate in screening, episode boundary,
      criterion selection, link reconstruction, judgment selection, or episode relation.
      The reviewer should identify that earliest divergence rather than treating every
      difference as one undifferentiated error.
    </p>

    <p>
      The research lead may advance the researcher to the codebook-assisted pass when
      the locked record demonstrates that the researcher can:
      <ul>
        <li>work independently under the stated access restrictions;</li>
        <li>read the complete question context before interpreting a response;</li>
        <li>screen for analyzable justification without screening on correctness;</li>
        <li>identify and preserve defensible episode boundaries;</li>
        <li>record <m>C</m>, <m>I</m>, and <m>J</m> without strengthening the response;</li>
        <li>apply link status and completeness separately;</li>
        <li>record chained and independent structure when present, with detected nesting noted;</li>
        <li>write useful uncertainty notes rather than force agreement;</li>
        <li>add no codebook-shaped columns or values; and</li>
        <li>preserve the unaided record for later comparison.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      Possible outcomes are:
      <dl width="narrow">
        <li>
          <title>Advance</title>
          <p>The blind record is procedurally valid and demonstrates stable independent extraction.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Targeted remediation</title>
          <p>Complete a new short blind set focused on one decision type, such as segmentation or implicit links.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Repeat the blind pass</title>
          <p>Complete a fresh packet when independence was compromised or several foundational decisions remain unstable.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Resolve a materials problem</title>
          <p>Repeat affected items when the packet, schema, or governing instructions were incomplete or inconsistent.</p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>

    <p>
      Corrections made during review belong in the assigned review record. They must not
      replace the locked blind submission. The difference between the two records is
      evidence about training, protocol clarity, and possible anchoring in later stages.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>